{
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "id": "h200-continuation-001:H01",
      "parents": [],
      "operation": "repair",
      "statement": "Continue C24 by testing whether its existing k5 verification controller should price the actual target and draft graph dispatches after DP synchronization. The installed objective already charges drafting, but that charge depends only on request count; supplied source analysis shows verification-budget changes can enlarge the draft graph. A synthetic CPU example changes the selected budget when this dependency is included, without establishing real latency. The proposed optimization corrects the existing cost model while preserving five-token proposals and probabilistic verification. First repair the offline measurement design and reconstruct legal dispatches. Retain C24's completed instrumentation-inconclusive result and GPU hold until qualified measurements establish whether reduced verification work outweighs draft padding, lost accepted progress and controller overhead.",
      "observed_bottleneck": "No expensive, removable bottleneck is established. Reported C24 observations include insufficient native-cycle coverage in one rank/window and unresolved CPU/GPU associations in five cells. Its target-occupied fractions cannot qualify savings. Static implementation evidence shows a request-count-indexed draft term. The supplied CPU artifact reports budgets 11 versus 3 under different invented cost assumptions; this establishes decision sensitivity, not preferable runtime behavior.",
      "mechanism": "Affected-event frequency: unknown; count cases where candidate verification budgets change synchronized target/draft descriptors and the selected policy. Component occupied time: target, draft, controller, sampling and communication must be measured separately using same-device unions. Exposed time: determine which changes shorten the replica's dependency chain; collective residence is not automatically removable computation. Expected net saving: compare complete cycle time per accepted output token, including changed progress, padding, controller work and any synchronization. Service transfer: only a subsequent frozen, unprofiled replay can establish median ITL benefit.",
      "proposed_change": "First audit the retained C24 traces and reader offline, preserving missing cycles and invalid associations rather than repairing them with invented timestamps. Specify a prospective measurement addendum. Reconstruct legal candidate budgets through installed varlen descriptors, DP maxima and draft sizing. Compare the existing solver with a solver using coupled dispatch costs and a locked static verification-prefix policy. Do not merely add another draft term. Implement a correction only after measured costs demonstrate consequential ranking errors. Determine whether existing metadata suffices; any additional collective must be explicitly priced. DeepInfer's runtime residual correction is a diagnostic comparator for calibration error, not an additional feature to bundle automatically.",
      "code_surface": "Attested vllm/v1/worker/gpu/spec_decode/adaptive_verification.py, especially build_cost_tables_from_curves and the budget objective; installed gpu/dp_utils.py, gpu/cudagraph_utils.py and gpu/spec_decode/speculator.py dispatch chain; DSpark confidence-head activation; current FlashMLA capability inventory. Use actual installed model_state.py and file hashes. DeepInfer mtp_varlen.hpp at 774ce244b87cc0eb8d4b94ab9f5de2ab22049f01 supplies a reference estimator, not a drop-in patch.",
      "applicability": "First priority for offline qualification, not first choice for execution. H200 SM90 eligibility is conditional on every current attention group supporting variable query lengths and the required graph behavior. Confidence-head metadata does not prove runtime activation or calibration. Research-design ready; GPU pilot held pending an independently reviewed instrumentation addendum; full experiment not ready. Preserve both TP1/DP4/EP4 replicas, Marlin, seq64, k5 and the frozen workload.",
      "predicted_direction": "Primary metric: frozen Mooncake 0.5x long-output ITL p50; define improvement as 1 minus treatment/control. Numerical low/base/high deltas are unknown. An unfavorable case occurs when draft expansion, controller overhead or reduced progress dominates. A favorable case requires frequent budget-ranking errors and measurable reductions in exposed cycle time per accepted token. Needed measurements are actual policy frequency, synchronized descriptors, qualified component and dependency timing, accepted progress and controller overhead. Neither synthetic costs nor target-occupied percentages provide a service estimate or bound.",
      "classification": "adaptation",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "explanation": "The startup objective is adequate because problematic dispatch combinations are rare or cost almost the same.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Replay all available profiling-phase states through both policies, then measure only frequently reached differing descriptor pairs. Record unchanged decisions and unchanged graph buckets as zero opportunity, not savings."
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Confidence miscalibration or context-dependent execution costs cause poor decisions more than draft-cost coupling.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Hold confidence arrays fixed when comparing cost models; separately compare predicted survival with actual acceptance and stratify cost residuals by context, graph shape and mixed-prefill status. Test static calibrated curves before adding an online estimator."
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Large target attribution reflects collective waiting or profiling perturbation.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Separate compute from communication and validate launch associations on all ranks. Compare matched instrumentation-on/off diagnostic brackets without interpreting unmatched brackets as an overhead estimate."
        }
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:installed-av-cost",
          "quote": "draft_cost_ms[len(req_ids)]",
          "relation": "supports"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:av-feasibility",
          "quote": "The same nominal k5 therefore does not imply unchanged draft execution cost when enabling AV.",
          "relation": "supports"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "artifact:continuation:av-cpu-counterexample",
          "quote": "\"coupled_cost_changes_decision\": true",
          "relation": "supports"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "artifact:continuation:c24-final",
          "quote": "**Decision: `instrumentation_inconclusive`; adaptive verification remains unqualified.**",
          "relation": "challenges"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:deepinfer-av",
          "quote": "const double time_ms = baseline_ms + slope_ms * graph_size + sampler_ms;",
          "relation": "context"
        }
      ],
      "novelty": {
        "scientific": "No scientific novelty claimed; this is verification-budget cost-model qualification.",
        "implementation": "Potential correction from request-count-only draft pricing to actual coupled dispatch pricing. Baseline inclusion of an equivalent correction must still be checked outside the supplied excerpt.",
        "workload": "Adaptation to H200 EP4 graph padding and native prefix traffic; no transfer of SM100 or Wide-EP performance claims.",
        "nearest_prior_art": "Installed vLLM adaptive verification and pinned DeepInfer MTPVarlen runtime-cost estimation.",
        "specific_delta": "Price a demonstrated downstream draft-cost dependency instead of assuming draft cost is constant across candidate verification budgets."
      },
      "prior_art_query": "verification budget coupled costs",
      "experiment": {
        "control": "Attested AV-off fixed-k5 baseline. For mechanism isolation, also evaluate the unchanged installed AV objective and a legal static verification-prefix policy selected on development states and then locked, all retaining five-token proposals. Use identical measured curves and states for policy comparisons; synthetic replay is not a service result.",
        "treatment": "Cheapest pilot: at most 8 engineering hours of offline reader, dispatch and objective checks, with zero GPU-hours. Stop before implementation if an equivalent correction exists or the dependency cannot occur. A later GPU pilot requires explicit resolution of the C24 measurement hold. Its maximum is one 60-minute baseline timing allocation, followed only on adequacy success by two 60-minute component/integration allocations for baseline and an AV harness. Compare stock, corrected and static policies on matched states within the harness. Total cap: 180 minutes on eight allocated GPUs, 24 GPU-hours, zero automatic retries. Use the exact diagnostic prefix and fixed capture schedule; exclude profiled results from the service frontier.",
        "metrics": "Policy disagreement frequency; target/draft descriptors before and after DP synchronization; per-rank eligible cycles; categorized device-busy union; target compute, collective, draft, sampler and controller occupied time; dependency-chain span; actual accepted output per cycle; confidence calibration and cost residuals. Retain all-rank draft/verify/accepted counters, exact token counts, KV/preemption, replay lag and request joins. Later service metrics include the frozen ITL statistic, separately defined request TPOT, TTFT p99, E2E p99, goodput and quality.",
        "supports_if": "Offline checks establish a real missing dependency and an implementable measurement design. GPU advancement additionally requires at least ten complete decode-only cycles per rank/window, valid launch associations, target/draft coverage and at least 95% categorized busy union, without weakening the old gate. Correctness must pass, differing decisions must have adequate native coverage, and blocked matched-state measurements must show positive net exposed savings per accepted token with workload incidence plausibly sufficient for the 2% objective. This permits consideration of one full attempt, not automatic execution. Full evaluation retains a positive screen, both 3% tail guards, and at least five alternating matched A/B confirmation blocks for a small gain; promotion requires the frozen 2% improvement criterion.",
        "rejects_if": "Stop for incompatible attention/graph support, incorrect compaction or verification, an already equivalent objective, or adequately measured unfavorable net tradeoffs. Reject the correction if the unchanged or locked static policy matches or beats it after controller and synchronization costs. Do not relabel rejection of this correction as proof that every AV policy fails.",
        "inconclusive_if": "Missing associations, insufficient cycles or budget disagreements, unavailable runtime confidence, ambiguous timing boundaries, excessive profiler perturbation, unstable cost curves, or any resource-cap overrun. Preserve the prior C24 verdict and defer advancement; no replacement run is automatic.",
        "held_out_condition": "Preserve native agent-f75 8K/1K and the full fixed-schedule Mooncake anchors at seq64. Hold out long-context, ragged and mixed-prefill states, plus cold/warm cache conditions, from cost-model selection. Use 1K/1K cache-isolated traffic only as a separate diagnostic. Seq128 remains outside this arm.",
        "correctness": "Apply relevant frozen G01-G05 and Q01-Q04 checks once their definitions are available. Test zero/intermediate/full budgets, empty and dummy ranks, lengths 1-6, DP disagreement, graph-boundary transitions, stale-confidence buffers, compaction/reallocation, bonus-token ownership, EOS, cancellation and recovery. Confirm real probabilistic rejection, watermark behavior and exact token accounting. Use controlled distributions and common-input verifier tests; acceptance diagnostics are not quality scores.",
        "cost": "Planning estimates, not measured effort: inspection/offline pilot 4-8 engineering hours, hard cap 8, GPU 0. Implementation/correctness is uncertain: an 8-hour feasibility checkpoint within a provisional 16-32 engineering-hour allocation, hard cap 32; defer if new distributed coordination or unsupported backends exceed it. Instrumentation, pilot operation and analysis: 8-16 engineering hours, cap 16; future GPU pilot cap 24 GPU-hours as three 60-minute node slots. Each slot includes approximately 26 minutes historical cold startup plus capture/calibration, warmup, measurement, export and cleanup; AV calibration duration is unknown, and exceeding a slot is inconclusive. If independently selected later: screen cap two 75-minute node slots = 20 GPU-hours, with 3-6 engineering hours; confirmation cap ten 75-minute slots = 100 GPU-hours, with 6-12 engineering hours; held-out allowance two 75-minute slots = 20 GPU-hours, with 2-4 engineering hours. These are alternative-candidate budget ceilings, not reservations or permission. At node price P per hour, monetary allocation cost is GPU-hours/8 times P. This response consumes zero GPU-hours."
      },
      "revision_reason": "Existing family C24 remains on hold after a completed instrumentation-inconclusive diagnostic; stale descriptions of pending timing are superseded. New evidence is the supplied installed objective, explicit target/draft dispatch coupling and synthetic budget counterexample. These justify an offline repair question, not reopening GPU execution. C03 remains screen-deferred and its patch is not combined. No immutable C24 hypothesis ID was supplied, so parents is empty rather than using the family label as a fabricated memory ID."
    },
    {
      "id": "h200-continuation-001:H02",
      "parents": [],
      "operation": "generate",
      "statement": "Test an isolated port of the unsharded DSpark Markov sampler fusion while retaining the existing H200 executor, Marlin experts and five-token proposal. Installed code exposes repeated embedding, logits, sampling and storage work at each sequential position; the supplied patch combines several of these operations while retaining the bias GEMM. Unlike C03's occasional graph-padding reduction, this could affect every eligible dense draft invocation. Eligibility is unresolved: upstream weight access requires specialized decode, and initialization excludes draft watermarking, top-k and adaptive verification. The baseline's watermark setting must therefore be traced to the actual draft object. Preserve the frozen semantics and reject a port that requires disabling them. Upstream GB300 bundle gains do not predict isolated H200 savings.",
      "observed_bottleneck": "Static installed-code evidence establishes the sequential loop, not its runtime frequency or cost. The PR authors report GB300 improvements for a bundle containing executor, fusion, sharding and graph changes; no isolated fusion effect is supplied. The frozen baseline enables watermarking, but whether draft_watermarker is non-null is unknown. No qualified H200 sampler timing exists in the supplied evidence.",
      "mechanism": "Affected-event frequency: eligible dense DSpark invocations, counted separately from top-k and fallback paths. Component occupied time: non-GEMM sampling kernels and intermediate memory operations across five positions; bias GEMMs and sequential dependence remain. Exposed time: measure the graph-contained chain against the existing graph-contained sampler, including scratch initialization and completion ordering. Expected net saving: removed kernel/glue work minus fusion, scratch and resource-contention costs. Service transfer: weight by native eligible invocations and verify actual accepted progress; unchanged token sampling is a correctness requirement, not an assumption.",
      "proposed_change": "First inspect installed membership and every constructor predicate, including draft watermark ownership, vocabulary mapping, logits layout, RNG precision and embedding access. If the frozen configuration qualifies, define a generic, opt-in unsharded adapter that preserves the installed markov_bias implementation and existing executor. Port only the sampling fusion after obtaining its complete reduction, launcher and scratch-lifetime implementation. Keep upstream specialized execution, vocabulary sharding, post-forward graph capture and AV outside this treatment. If draft watermarking is active or another semantic gate cannot be preserved, stop this scoped port rather than switching the feature off.",
      "code_surface": "Attested vllm/v1/worker/gpu/spec_decode/dspark/speculator.py::_sample_sequential and _sample_logits; proposed dspark/fused_sample.py; model Markov embedding accessor and draft-logit cache interfaces. The supplied selected patch is truncated, and its specialized-decode weight gate requires an explicit adapter. Actual installed model_state.py governs graph integration.",
      "applicability": "Second conditional qualification priority, subject to a cheap gate check. Targets TP1 unsharded sampling on SM90 with AV off. FP64 Gumbel is not automatically excluded from the initial fused sampler; its separate sharded restriction must not be misapplied. Research ready; implementation and GPU pilot readiness pending gate, full-source and SM90 checks; full experiment not ready.",
      "predicted_direction": "Primary metric: frozen Mooncake 0.5x long-output ITL p50. Low/base/high numerical deltas are unknown. The low case is no activation or regression from fused-kernel contention and scratch work. A favorable case requires frequent activation and material shortening of the already captured sampling chain. Measure graph-to-graph component differences, actual invocation frequency, critical-path exposure and acceptance before estimating any service delta. The PR's bundled 6.4% batch-one result is an author claim on GB300, not an H200 prediction.",
      "classification": "upstream_port",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "explanation": "Existing CUDA graphs already hide most launch overhead, leaving little removable device work.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Compare both samplers inside matched existing FULL graphs. Do not use Python eager execution as the performance control. Separate device chain duration from host submission time."
        },
        {
          "explanation": "The PR's gains predominantly come from specialized draft execution, sharding or post-forward capture.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Keep those features absent in both local arms and change only the unsharded sampling path."
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Upstream parity tests conceal numerical differences because their Markov weights are integer-valued.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Use actual checkpoint weights and controlled sampler inputs, including rounding-sensitive logits, ties, padding and repeated graph replay; compare cached draft probabilities and RNG-keyed results."
        }
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:installed-dspark",
          "quote": "for i in range(n_spec):",
          "relation": "supports"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:vllm-project/vllm:61869:selected-patch",
          "quote": "The fused path keeps the bias GEMM",
          "relation": "supports"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:fused-sampler-gates",
          "quote": "if not self.specialized_decode_requested:",
          "relation": "challenges"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:fused-sampler-gates",
          "quote": "and self.draft_watermarker is None",
          "relation": "challenges"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "github:vllm-project/vllm:61869",
          "quote": "The Markov weights are integer-valued",
          "relation": "challenges"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "runs/c03-mechanism-review-001/REPORT.md",
          "quote": "Rows are not milliseconds.",
          "relation": "context"
        }
      ],
      "novelty": {
        "scientific": "Known kernel fusion; no scientific novelty claimed.",
        "implementation": "A narrow unsharded SM90 adapter independent of the PR's specialized executor, conditional on preserving all active baseline semantics.",
        "workload": "Native H200 seq64 DSpark traffic rather than the upstream GB300 specialized-decode bundle.",
        "nearest_prior_art": "vLLM PR61869's fused Markov sampler and the installed sequential sampler.",
        "specific_delta": "Reduce repeated sampling operations on real draft tokens without changing graph-sizing policy, proposal width, expert backend or target execution."
      },
      "prior_art_query": "DSpark Markov sampler fusion",
      "experiment": {
        "control": "Unmodified installed dense DSpark sampler inside its existing FULL graphs, with identical real weights, logits, RNG keys, graph sizes and scratch-lifetime conditions. Preserve the complete serving baseline for later integration; compare graph against graph, not against uncaptured eager Python.",
        "treatment": "Cheapest pilot: a maximum 4-hour CPU/source gate audit, zero GPU-hours. Stop if preserving the active watermark or other required semantics is incompatible. After a reviewed adapter and complete source are available, cap a standalone SM90 compile/correctness/component pilot at 45 minutes of eight-GPU reservation = 6 GPU-hours. Only a passing component result permits two 60-minute all-rank integration allocations = 16 GPU-hours, using a fixed diagnostic prefix and actual probabilistic acceptance. Total future pilot cap: 165 minutes, 22 GPU-hours, no automatic retries. Treat unsupported configurations as explicit fallbacks and record their frequency.",
        "metrics": "All-rank dense/top-k/fallback path counts; eligible invocation and sampling-position counts; graph-contained sampler duration, bias GEMM duration, scratch work, host submission and exposed dependency-chain time. Record logits-cache equality, sampled-token equality under controlled inputs, actual draft/accepted counters and output-token accounting. Later measure frozen ITL, request TPOT, TTFT/E2E tails, goodput, replay lag, KV/preemption and quality.",
        "supports_if": "The baseline predicates are satisfied without changing the contract, SM90 correctness passes, and paired graph replay with real weights shows positive net sampler savings across native shapes. Integration must demonstrate legal activation on all eight ranks, at least twenty affected cycles per rank across two predeclared windows, adequate phase-only opportunity accounting and no accepted-progress loss attributable to the port. Advancement also requires qualified critical-path evidence making the 2% service objective plausible. A full attempt retains the positive screen, 3% TTFT/E2E tail guards, at least five alternating paired confirmation blocks and the frozen 2% promotion requirement.",
        "rejects_if": "Stop for an incompatible active watermark/top-k/layout requirement, inability to detach specialization safely, sampling or cache mismatch, unsafe scratch reuse, or adequately measured nonpositive exposed savings. Reject an activation-only result as insufficient for allocation. Do not weaken probabilistic decoding or watermark semantics to obtain a benchmark.",
        "inconclusive_if": "Complete kernel/launcher source is unavailable, runtime gates remain unknown, real-weight fixtures are missing, fewer than the required native opportunities are observed, timing associations fail, results are dominated by compilation, or any time cap is reached. Defer without a service attempt.",
        "held_out_condition": "Hold out ragged and zero-rank batches, graph-boundary shapes, long-context agent turns, mixed prefill/decode and cold/warm cache traffic. Preserve native agent-f75 8K/1K and fixed Mooncake anchors at seq64. Keep 1K/1K cache-isolated tests diagnostic and separate from the primary score.",
        "correctness": "Use relevant G01-G05 and Q01-Q04 checks, including controlled real-weight sampling at temperatures 0, 0.6 and 1; configured FP32/FP64 Gumbel behavior; RNG seed/step/position identity; vocabulary remapping; dtype rounding; first-maximum and exceptional-value handling where defined by the reference; padded and empty rows; anchor layout; draft-logit cache contents; arrival-counter ordering and reset; repeated graph replay; cancellation, EOS and recovery. Verify actual rejection sampling and watermark preservation. Upstream integer-weight parity and acceptance length do not substitute for these tests or the quality subset.",
        "cost": "Uncertain planning estimates: inspection 2-4 engineering hours, hard cap 4, GPU 0. Implementation/correctness 16-32 engineering hours only if the isolated adapter is feasible, hard cap 32 with an 8-hour feasibility stop; full launcher availability, watermark interaction and SM90 synchronization are unknown. Instrumentation/pilot analysis 6-10 engineering hours, cap 10. Standalone pilot 45 minutes/6 GPU-hours includes process startup, compilation, graph capture and correctness/timing; two integration slots add 120 minutes/16 GPU-hours, including historical approximately 26-minute startup per arm, warmup, capture, measurement, export and cleanup. Total pilot ceiling 22 GPU-hours. Conditional later screen: 3-6 engineering hours and 20 GPU-hours; five-pair confirmation: 6-12 engineering hours and 100 GPU-hours; held-out allowance: 2-4 engineering hours and 20 GPU-hours, using 75-minute all-eight-GPU arm caps. No retries are budgeted. Price is P times GPU-hours/8; fixed-load throughput cannot establish cost-per-token or capacity gains. No GPU work occurs in this response."
      },
      "revision_reason": "Existing family C03 remains held after its unfavorable service screen. This is a distinct mechanism affecting sequential sampling rather than occasional draft-capacity reductions, so it does not reopen or rename C03. New supplied PR61869 code provides an implementation lead, while its exact specialization and watermark gates narrow the proposal from simple activation to a conditional isolated port. C24 remains separate and AV stays off in this candidate; Q01 sampling qualification remains a prerequisite."
    },
    {
      "id": "h200-continuation-001:H03",
      "parents": [],
      "operation": "generate",
      "statement": "Check whether the installed DeepSeek learned router reaches the FP32-weight GEMM branch targeted by the proposed Hopper BF16x3 kernel, then port only that kernel and its qualified dispatch if it does. The supplied nominal model constructs GateLinear with FP32 output, which does not establish FP32 weights or active GEMM execution; early hash-routing layers further limit exposure. This makes a short dtype, loader and dispatch audit the first decision point. If eligible, faster routing could shorten repeated target verification steps without replacing Marlin experts or communication. Count padded token rows rather than requests. Upstream H200 measurements support a hardware-specific lead, but their different models, shapes and workloads provide no DeepSeek service estimate.",
      "observed_bottleneck": "No local router bottleneck or eligible weight dtype is observed. Nominal source establishes GateLinear construction and hash-routing state only. PR61903 authors report H200 kernel and other-model improvements, but no DeepSeek-V4.1 H200 result or kernel patch is supplied. Active installed bytes, learned-router layer count, shapes and time share are unknown.",
      "mechanism": "Affected-event frequency: learned-router calls whose loaded weight/activation dtypes, shape, padded token rows and dispatch settings qualify; exclude hash bypasses and unaffected small-row paths. Component occupied time: the eligible router GEMM and any conversion or dispatch overhead. Exposed time: the portion on the target/draft dependency chain, not summed GPU time across ranks. Expected net saving: eligible call frequency times measured exposed per-call reduction, minus conversion/JIT/dispatch costs; treat this as component accounting, not a median-ITL prediction. Service transfer: routing changes must preserve quality and pass the frozen unprofiled comparison.",
      "proposed_change": "Inspect actual installed GateLinear forward/loader code, parameter metadata, dtype conversions, shape-specific dispatch, batch-invariant settings and any equivalent SM90 implementation. Distinguish target from draft and learned from hash routing. If weights are BF16, the path is bypassed, or the optimization is already active, stop. If eligibility remains plausible, obtain and review the exact PR kernel and warmup implementation before defining a minimal isolated port. Preserve Marlin, activation formats outside the router, routing policy, allgather_reducescatter and graph settings.",
      "code_surface": "Actual installed counterparts of vllm/models/deepseek_v4/nvidia/model.py and GateLinear's loader/forward/JIT dispatch, including V4.1 inheritance. The supplied model.py is nominal upstream source, not an attested installed router. PR61903 names torch.ops.vllm.sm90_bf16x3_router_gemm and VllmJitKernel warmup, but its implementation is absent from the supplied packet.",
      "applicability": "Cheap eligibility audit ready; implementation and GPU pilot deferred pending loaded-dtype, active-branch and complete-source evidence. The PR's 24-token cutoff applies to its specified GEMV shapes and must not be generalized blindly to DeepSeek shapes. H200 SM90 matches the claimed hardware class, but model eligibility is unknown. Full experiment not ready.",
      "predicted_direction": "Primary metric: frozen Mooncake 0.5x long-output ITL p50. Numerical low/base/high deltas are unknown. Ineligible or already optimized routing gives zero baseline delta; a qualifying path can still yield negligible service savings if rare or unexposed. A favorable case requires repeated costly learned-router calls at native padded row counts and preserved routing quality. Measure dtype/branch coverage, local shapes, graph-contained router time, exposed dependency time and downstream routing changes before estimating any gain. Other-model H200 speedups are not transferred.",
      "classification": "upstream_port",
      "alternatives": [
        {
          "explanation": "FP32 output is mistaken for FP32 weights, or hash routing bypasses the GEMM.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Trace checkpoint metadata through installed loading/casts and forward dispatch, then attest actual parameter dtypes and path hits during any later qualified pilot. Count affected layers separately."
        },
        {
          "explanation": "The upstream benefit depends on different router shapes, cold-L2 conditions or larger token counts.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Benchmark exact local shapes and native padded rows under both cold-weight and steady graph-replay conditions; retain unaffected shapes as controls."
        },
        {
          "explanation": "A faster GEMM changes near-tied expert selections enough to alter downstream work or quality.",
          "distinguishing_test": "Compare router logits, top-k identities, routing weights and margins against the installed path on real activations; evaluate full-layer outputs and a frozen changed-math quality subset before timing service."
        }
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:pinned-router",
          "quote": "out_dtype=torch.float32,",
          "relation": "challenges"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "code:continuation:pinned-router",
          "quote": "is_hash_moe = extract_layer_index(prefix) < num_hash_layers",
          "relation": "context"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "github:vllm-project/vllm:61903",
          "quote": "This PR adds a CuteDSL BF16x3 kernel for SM90 behind the existing BF16x3 tier.",
          "relation": "supports"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "github:vllm-project/vllm:61903",
          "quote": "No change for 24 or fewer tokens",
          "relation": "challenges"
        }
      ],
      "novelty": {
        "scientific": "Known mixed-component approximation for FP32-weight GEMM; no scientific novelty claimed.",
        "implementation": "Conditional minimal port of an existing SM90 proposal with DeepSeek-specific eligibility and warmup coverage.",
        "workload": "Measure native k5 verification token rows and hash/learned-router exposure in the existing EP4 service.",
        "nearest_prior_art": "vLLM PR61903 and existing GateLinear GEMV/SGEMM dispatch.",
        "specific_delta": "Replace only a demonstrated eligible router SGEMM, leaving expert GEMMs and transport unchanged."
      },
      "prior_art_query": "SM90 BF16x3 router GEMM",
      "experiment": {
        "control": "Actual installed GateLinear dispatch on identical loaded weights and recorded activations. Include its existing small-row GEMV path as a static comparator wherever legal; do not force a slower SGEMM control. Keep full serving configuration unchanged.",
        "treatment": "Cheapest pilot: maximum 3 engineering hours of CPU/source/metadata eligibility inspection, zero GPU-hours. Stop for an absent or already optimized path. Only after exact kernel review and correctness design, cap a standalone shape/graph test at 30 minutes of eight-GPU reservation = 4 GPU-hours. A passing result permits two 60-minute all-rank integration allocations = 16 GPU-hours. Total future pilot cap: 150 minutes, 20 GPU-hours, zero automatic retries. Use fixed native diagnostic traffic and exclude profiled runs from the service frontier.",
        "metrics": "Loaded weight and activation dtypes; target/draft role; learned/hash branch; native and padded rows; affected call frequency by layer/rank; graph-contained per-call duration and critical-path exposure; conversion/dispatch/JIT overhead; routing-logit error, top-k identities and routing weights. Integration retains all-rank speculative counters, exact tokens, KV/preemption and replay fidelity. Later evaluate frozen ITL, request TPOT, TTFT/E2E tails, goodput and changed-math quality.",
        "supports_if": "An absent eligible branch is established, exact local shapes compile and pass numerical/graph tests, and matched graph replay shows net savings against the fastest legal installed path. Require at least twenty eligible learned-router invocations per affected role/rank in each of two predeclared integration windows, with complete layer-role attribution and qualified timing. Native-frequency/exposure accounting must make the 2% objective plausible; kernel speed alone does not suffice. Any full attempt retains the positive screen, 3% tail guards, five alternating matched confirmation pairs for small gains and the frozen 2% promotion requirement.",
        "rejects_if": "Stop if weights are ineligible, the GEMM is bypassed, an equivalent optimization is already active, local shapes are unsupported within scope, correctness/quality fails, or adequately measured net exposed savings are unfavorable or immaterial for allocation. Do not change router precision or the workload merely to activate the candidate.",
        "inconclusive_if": "Loaded dtype cannot be established offline, the exact proposed implementation remains unavailable, active branch telemetry is missing, opportunity coverage is inadequate, timing attribution fails, or compilation/startup exhausts a cap. Such outcomes defer the candidate rather than treating missing hits as zero opportunity.",
